            "selectCount": 1,
            "bodyHtml": "<p>Complete the notes below. Write <strong>ONE WORD ONLY</strong> for each answer.</p><div><div class=\"notes-title\"><strong>Soundscapes</strong></div><p class=\"sub-head\">Soundscape – the sound environment</p><ul><li>the term was first used by a Canadian, Murray Schafer, a well-known {{31}}</li><li>the soundscape is not just noise, but has {{32}} which can be interpreted</li><li>divisions of the soundscape <ul><li>biophony – created by living organisms, e.g. {{33}}</li><li>geophony – caused by non-biological forces, e.g. {{34}}</li><li>anthrophony – made by humans</li></ul></li></ul><p class=\"sub-head\">Conclusions of Bernie Krause</p><ul><li>every sound in nature has a unique {{35}}</li><li>the sounds of nature are harmonious in {{36}} and frequency</li></ul><p class=\"sub-head\">Soundscape changes can be</p><ul><li>an indication of a change in the {{37}} of an environment</li><li>a warning of the presence of different types of {{38}}, e.g. insecticides</li></ul><p>Equipment used in the study of soundscapes</p><ul><li>acoustic recorders – data are used to create {{39}} in 3-D</li></ul><p class=\"sub-head\">Most common query</p><p class=\"sub-head\">What is the noisiest creature?</p><p>Answer: probably a particular kind of {{40}} which lives in the ocean</p></div>",
